Why Caching Was Needed

Initially, graph construction recalculated:

Haversine distances
adjacency lists
edge connections

for every request.

This caused:

repeated CPU computation
repeated graph reconstruction
unnecessary traversal overhead

Since metro topology is mostly static, rebuilding the graph per request was inefficient.

Current Caching Architecture

The graph is cached in-memory at the application layer using a singleton Spring service.

@Service
public class GraphService

Spring services are singleton by default, so:

one graph instance exists for the entire server lifecycle
graph persists across incoming requests
distances are computed only once
Cached Runtime Structure

The following data is cached:

Adjacency List Graph
Precomputed Edge Distances
Branch Connections
Station Topology

The cache exists only in server memory and is not persisted to MongoDB.

Graph Initialization Flow
Application Start
→ Load Station Data From MongoDB
→ Build Graph
→ Compute Haversine Distances
→ Store Distances In Edge Objects
→ Cache Entire Graph In Memory
Persistent Runtime Behavior

The following logic ensures persistence across requests:

if (graphInitialized && cachedGraph != null)

If the graph already exists:

graph rebuilding is skipped
cached graph is immediately returned

This reduces route computation overhead significantly.